{"step": 2, "unit": "model-inference/src/main/application/models/pytorch_pairwise_ranker.py::PairwiseRankerModel.forward", "what_it_does": "Builds a graph that concatenates the query with each of two documents, passes each pair through a shared linear and sigmoid, concatenates the two results into a compare linear, and returns a sigmoid score; main reaches it only through torch.onnx.export, which writes pairwise_ranker.onnx.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [16, 19], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The waste is real: the query half of query_doc_transform is recomputed on lines 16-17 and again on 18-19. The repair (apply the linear to the query once, then add the document part, i.e. split the weight into query and document columns) reassociates the floating-point sums, so forward outputs are not bit-identical, and the exported graph changes, so the written pairwise_ranker.onnx differs. Checked: grep shows PairwiseRankerModel is referenced only in this file, forward is reached only via torch.onnx.export in main, and the Java side (ModelsTest.java lines 23-26, MyPostProcessingSearcher.java line 32) loads the pairwise_ranker model from the models directory that holds this artifact."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "multilingual-search/ext/evaluate.py::search", "what_it_does": "For one query group it takes the query text from the group rows (last row wins), posts one hybrid search request to the endpoint, appends one record per hit to the global responses list, and returns None.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [12, 13], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Replace the for loop with `query = query_group['query'].iloc[-1]`. The loop leaves the last row's value in query, which is exactly iloc[-1]; include_groups=False leaves only the query column in the group frame, so the value and its type match (str, or the same numpy scalar for a numeric column). Mechanism: iterrows builds one pandas Series per row inside a Python loop, so with one row per group this removes one avoidable Series construction per query, a small saving next to the HTTP round trip. Groups from groupby.apply are never empty, so the empty-group case cannot arise. Sole-caller evidence: search is referenced only at line 75 of this file, inside groupby.apply, and its None return is discarded, so no other code reads the loop variable."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "multilingual-search/ext/evaluate.py::main", "what_it_does": "Parses arguments, opens one requests Session with retries, reads the query TSV, runs search over each query_id group in groupby order, then writes the accumulated hits to <ranking>.run.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [75], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Parallelising the apply would have to change search (it appends to the global responses list and returns None) and main, so the edit reaches beyond the unit. Threads appending in completion order would change the row order of the written .run file unless results are re-collected in group order, and the shared requests.Session has no thread-safety guarantee. The step-1 note makes this an energy lever only if idle time costs power, which is not established, so the change is wall-clock only. Checked: search is referenced only at line 75 of this file."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "use-case-shopping/convert_meta.py::main", "what_it_does": "Reads product lines from stdin, converts each parsed dict to a Vespa put document with process, skips lines that raise, and prints the whole list as indented JSON to stdout (README line 91 redirects it to feed_items.json), with a count written to stderr.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [57, 67], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Two repairs share this label and both fail. Line 57: iterating sys.stdin instead of readlines gives identical records and the same uncaught UnicodeDecodeError on valid input, but on malformed UTF-8 the readlines version fails before any 'Processed N lines so far' stderr record, while streaming would emit those records for earlier lines first, so the log records differ on that input. Line 67: dropping indent=2 changes the bytes of the printed JSON, which the README writes to feed_items.json, a written-file change. Both fail the same-output contract, so the suspicion is rejected."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "use-case-shopping/convert_reviews.py::main", "what_it_does": "Reads review lines from stdin, converts each to a Vespa put document with process (returning None for illegal characters), counts skipped lines, and prints the list as indented JSON to stdout (README line 97 writes it to feed_reviews.json).", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [46, 58], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Same two repairs and same checks as convert_meta main. Line 46 (iterate sys.stdin instead of readlines) changes stderr progress records on malformed UTF-8 input, because the readlines version fails before writing any progress line. Line 58 (drop indent=2) changes the bytes of the JSON written to feed_reviews.json, a written-file change. Rejected."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "test/pseudo_terminal.py::Log.flush", "what_it_does": "Is the flush method of the file-like Log object that pexpect calls after every write of a read chunk; it flushes sys.stdout so command output reaches the console as it arrives.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [35, 36], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "pexpect spawnbase _log (site-packages/pexpect/spawnbase.py lines 127-134) calls logfile_read.write and then .flush() on every chunk, so the method must stay. The only repair is an empty body, which changes when stdout bytes are emitted: when stdout is a pipe or file it is block-buffered, so live output is delayed and its interleaving with the ERROR lines test.py writes to stderr can change. Output timing and ordering are part of the contract. The saving is one flush call per read chunk, which is negligible. Rejected."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "test/test.py::process_liquid", "what_it_does": "Applies each regex in the module-level liquid_transforms dict via re.sub, in insertion order, to one command block and returns the rewritten text; parse_cmds calls it once per block.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [175, 179], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The per-key passes are sequential by design: each substitution sees the output of the previous one, so merging them into one alternation can create or hide matches for a later key and is not identical on every input. Precompiling changes the stored values of the module-level liquid_transforms (grep: read only here, written in load_liquid_transforms) and gains almost nothing, because re.sub already caches compiled patterns, so the only saving is a cache lookup per key per block. Rejected."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "test/test.py::process_page", "what_it_does": "Parses the HTML page into a script dict, prints a section header and the whole script as indented JSON to stdout on every run, then executes the script via exec_script.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [285, 289], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The printed script is printed output, which is part of the contract. The print at line 289 is not gated by verbose, so removing it or gating it changes stdout in the default run; those are the only repairs, and both fail the printed-output check. Its cost (one json.dumps of a small dict per run) is also tiny next to command execution."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "test/test.py::run_url", "what_it_does": "Prints a header, fetches each comma-separated entry (urlopen for http entries, local file read otherwise), concatenates the bytes in order, and passes the result to process_page.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [304, 315], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Keep-alive would need http.client or a session object, which changes exception types (urllib raises HTTPError on non-2xx, and run_config at line 330 catches only RuntimeError), so the contract breaks. De-duplication fails two ways: within one comma list, repeats are concatenated on purpose and dropping them changes the bytes passed to process_page and so the executed script; a cache across run_url calls needs new module state and would return stale content if a remote page changed mid-run. Checked: test/_test_config.yml lists distinct entries, all local paths rather than http, so urlopen and repeats do not occur in the configured run. Rejected."}]}

