{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/mind_data.py::MindData.sample_training_data", "what_it_does": "Subsamples train impressions with prob, expands each kept impression into positive and sampled negative rows via add_impressions, and wraps the resulting lists in a TensorDataset and a RandomSampler DataLoader for one epoch.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [144, 157], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The per-call rebuild is required: each call draws new random.random() subsample decisions and random.sample negatives, so the epoch data differs per call. Caching the dataset or tensors would skip those draws, changing the training data and the global random state that later code sees. The per-sample dict lookups the suspicion mentions are in add_impressions (lines 189-200, unit 3), not in lines 144-157, and the torch.LongTensor conversions in lines 148-154 have no output-identical cheaper form inside this unit."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/mind_data.py::MindData.add_impressions", "what_it_does": "For one train impression, pairs each clicked candidate with up to num_negatives randomly sampled skipped candidates and appends user, news, category, subcategory, entity and label values to the shared lists.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [181, 201], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Lines 189-191 and 198-200 each subscript self.news_content[idx] three times (category_index, subcategory_index, entity_index), costing three dict probes and three self attribute loads per generated row. Edit: in each loop body bind content = self.news_content[idx] once and take the three fields from content. Appended values and order are identical, a missing index still raises KeyError at the same first access, and the only caller is sample_training_data (mind_data.py:146), so no other code sees the change. Mechanism: two fewer dict probes and attribute loads per row."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/mind_data.py::MindData.sample_valid_data", "what_it_does": "Builds a list of per-impression tensor tuples for the evaluation loop, optionally over the train set, keeping only impressions whose labels are mixed and subsampling with prob.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [159, 179], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Edit: when train is True, keep a cache dict on self keyed by impression index holding the built tensor tuple (or a marker for impressions filtered out), while still calling random.random() once per impression in the same order so the RNG draws are unchanged; later calls reuse the cached tuples instead of rebuilding the category, subcategory and entity lists and the five torch tensors. Mechanism: after the first epoch the per-impression list comprehensions and tensor constructions are skipped. Sole-caller evidence: sample_valid_data is called only at train_mf.py:85 and train_cold_start.py:124, and both eval_model loops unpack the tuple, call the model and detach().numpy() without writing into the inputs; the cache attribute is new, so no other code reads it. Caveat: the cache keeps the train tensors alive for the process lifetime, a memory cost."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/mind_data.py::MindData.read_news", "what_it_does": "Parses each news TSV row, collects WikidataIds from title and abstract entities, and stores category, subcategory and the first two entity indices for each news id in news_content, registering every looked-up id in the MindData maps.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [63, 74], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Dropping the second entity column is not a pure removal: lookup_entity_index on the second id inserts it into entity_map, which entities() exposes. train_cold_start.py:205 uses len(entities()) as the nn.Embedding size (train_cold_start.py:44), and the indices of later entities depend on insertion order, so num_entities and the entity indices change. The model reads only column 0 (train_cold_start.py:68), but the registration side effect decides the rejection."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/evaluate.py::calc_metrics", "what_it_does": "Reads the full impressions file into dicts, draws sample_size impressions with random.sample, scores each by calling Vespa for user and candidate news, and returns auc, mrr and ndcg metrics.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [86], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Sampling before parsing keeps the RNG check intact: random.sample(range(n), k) draws the same indices as random.sample(list_of_n, k), and read_impressions_file has a single caller at evaluate.py:84. The rejection comes from the exception check: the repair stops validating unsampled rows, so a malformed row the original raises on (a token without a dash giving ValueError at the split, a non-integer label, or a short row giving None) no longer raises unless it is sampled. Keeping the original exception means validating every row, which is the cost being removed."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/evaluate.py::read_impressions_file", "what_it_does": "Parses every row of an impressions TSV into a dict with user_id, news_ids and labels and returns the complete list.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [25, 47], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The return value must contain every row, since calc_metrics uses len, enumerate and random.sample on it (evaluate.py:84-88), so no parsing can be skipped inside this function without changing the return contract. The only edit that skips parsing returns a sampled subset, which is the same edit as label a of evaluate.py::calc_metrics and fails the same exception check on unsampled malformed rows."}]}
