{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/create_bert_embeddings.py::create_embeddings", "what_it_does": "For each unique docid in a MIND news TSV, tokenizes title and abstract, runs one BertModel forward at batch size 1, and writes the first-token 512-float vector as a tab-separated line to the output file, printing progress about every 1 percent.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [51, 59], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Add `import torch` (the file does not import it) and wrap the model call at line 58 in `with torch.no_grad():`, leaving the tokenizer call, write calls and print unchanged. Forward values are identical because no_grad only disables autograd recording, and the mechanism is that no graph nodes or saved activations are allocated for each of the per-document forwards. Only this function is touched, and it is called only from main lines 77-78 in the same file."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [59], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Replace `outputs[0].tolist()[0][0]` with `outputs[0][0][0].tolist()`, which yields the same list of 512 Python floats, so write_embedding gets an equal list and the bytes are unchanged. The original converts the whole 1 x seq_len x 512 last_hidden_state (up to 51,200 values) to Python floats and keeps 512 of them, while the edit converts only the 512 it uses."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/create_bert_embeddings.py::write_embedding", "what_it_does": "Writes a tab followed by the embedding values joined with commas, each formatted with %.4f, to an open file handle, with no trailing newline.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [68, 73], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Replace the loop with one f.write call that writes a tab character followed by ','.join over '%.4f' % num for each num. The bytes are identical, including the empty-embedding case, which still yields only the tab. The original issues about 1,000 f.write calls per document through the text wrapper, and the edit issues one; the only caller is create_embeddings line 59 in the same file."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/create_category_ctrs.py::write_news_category_ctr_update", "what_it_does": "Streams a JSON array to news_category_ctr_update.json with one Vespa update per news id, each assigning the global category CTR reference and a one-cell category_tensor for that item's category.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [76], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Keep a dict inside the function from category to the formatted category_tensor string, formatting only on a miss, so the output bytes are the same. The '%' formatting of an identical string then runs once per distinct category instead of once per news item. The impact is low because the writes on lines 77-81 are untouched and dominate."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/convert_embeddings_to_vespa_format.py::convert_user_embeddings", "what_it_does": "Reads the user embeddings TSV into numpy float32 vectors and writes a Vespa feed JSON array with one put per user, each carrying its embedding formatted to six decimals.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [39], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Iterate over embedding.tolist() on line 39, giving ','.join(['%.6f' % v for v in embedding.tolist()]). The bytes are identical because '%f' on a float32 converts exactly to the same Python float that tolist returns. The mechanism is that iterating the numpy array creates a numpy scalar per element, while tolist converts the whole vector in one call; the saving is small and the script runs once, so the impact is low."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/convert_embeddings_to_vespa_format.py::convert_news_embeddings", "what_it_does": "Reads the news embeddings TSV and writes a Vespa update JSON array with one assign per news id, each carrying its embedding formatted to six decimals.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [53], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Same edit as label a of convert_user_embeddings: iterate over embedding.tolist() in the join on line 53. The bytes are identical for the same float32-to-float reason, and the mechanism and low impact are as stated there."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/train_mf.py::main", "what_it_does": "Seeds the RNGs, loads MIND data, builds the MF model, trains it, evaluates the final model on train and valid impressions printing both metric dicts, then saves the user and news embedding TSVs.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [145, 146], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Make eval_model return its metrics after its unchanged print, and have train_model return the epoch index and (train, valid) metrics of its last 10-epoch evaluation. In main, when that epoch equals epochs - 1 (the default 100 gives 99), print the two stored dicts in the same order instead of calling eval_model again; otherwise run the two evals as now, so the printed lines are identical. The saving is one full train-set pass and one valid pass over identical weights. Beyond the unit, grep shows train_model is called only at train_mf.py line 142 and eval_model only at lines 80, 81, 145 and 146, with no other importer; no random draws follow these calls, since save_embeddings uses none."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/train_mf.py::train_model", "what_it_does": "Runs the MF training loop with fresh negative samples each epoch, and every tenth epoch prints a train-set metrics dict and a validation metrics dict.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [79, 81], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The train=True evaluation's dict is printed output, so dropping it or subsampling it changes what is printed. Also, sample_valid_data in mind_data.py calls random.random() once per impression, which advances the global random stream used by the next epoch's sample_training_data, so removing or moving the call changes the trained weights. No repair keeps both the printed value and the training trajectory."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/train_mf.py::eval_model", "what_it_does": "Runs the MF model on each impression from sample_valid_data under no_grad, collects per-impression predictions and labels, and prints group AUC, MRR, NDCG@5 and NDCG@10.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [91, 95], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Batching would run one forward over all impressions and split the output afterwards, which changes the shapes of the elementwise multiply and of the sum over dim 1. The printed metrics are full-precision floats rounded to 4 places, so a last-bit change can flip a printed digit at a rounding boundary. Bitwise equality cannot be shown by reading, and no run was done, so the repair is not verified; it would also hold all impressions in memory."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/train_cold_start.py::train_model", "what_it_does": "Runs the content-based training loop; after every epoch it prints train and validation metric dicts via eval_model and then rewrites user_embeddings.tsv and news_embeddings.tsv in data_dir through save_embeddings.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [116, 120], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Accepted for the embedding rewrite part only: guard line 120 with `if epoch == epochs - 1:` so save_embeddings runs once, after the final epoch's evals in the same position. The evals on lines 116-117 are kept, because they print and consume the global random stream through sample_valid_data. The final files are byte-identical, and epochs=0 still writes nothing. The mechanism is that epochs-1 full recomputations of the user and news tables (BERT transform and content layers over all news) and their writes stop. Beyond the unit, grep shows save_embeddings is called only at line 120, train_model only at line 214 in main, save_embeddings uses no random numbers, and nothing in the repo reads the two output files during the run. Caveat: a run killed before its last epoch leaves no intermediate snapshot, which the original would have left."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/train_cold_start.py::eval_model", "what_it_does": "Runs the content-based model on each impression from sample_valid_data under no_grad, collects per-impression predictions and labels, and prints group AUC, MRR, NDCG@5 and NDCG@10.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [130, 135], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Batching the impressions, or computing all news embeddings once and indexing them, changes the shapes of the Linear layers (news_bert_transform, news_content_transform) and of the sum reduction, so the float outputs cannot be shown bit-identical to the per-impression path. The printed metrics are full-precision values rounded to 4 places, so a last-bit change can still flip a printed digit. No run was done, so the repair is not verified."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/train_cold_start.py::save_embeddings", "what_it_does": "Computes the full user embedding table and the full news embedding table from the model, using the news content tensors, and writes them to data_dir as user_embeddings.tsv and news_embeddings.tsv.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [146, 160], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The full recomputation and both writes are this function's entire job whenever it is called, and every edit inside its body keeps those same computations and writes. Removing the waste means changing whether the function is called, which is the caller's decision at train_cold_start.py line 120 (the repair is under unit news/src/python/train_cold_start.py::train_model label a). No concrete edit to this unit removes the waste, so it is rejected here."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/train_cold_start.py::write_embeddings", "what_it_does": "Writes one line per id in the map to data_dir/file_name: the id, a tab, then the row's values formatted with %.6f and comma-joined.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [146, 160], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Convert once before the loop with rows = embeddings.tolist(), and replace embeddings[index].tolist() on line 160 with rows[index]; the %.6f join stays the same. The bytes are identical because tolist on the matrix gives the same float values row by row, and a bad index still raises IndexError. The mechanism is that the per-row tensor view and tolist dispatch stop, while the float conversions stay the same count; the per-float format loop is kept because any formatting change would change the bytes. The only caller is save_embeddings lines 153-154 in this file, and peak memory rises because all rows are converted up front."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "news/src/python/train_cold_start.py::train_epoch", "what_it_does": "Runs one epoch of minibatch training with BCE loss, accumulating the batch loss tensors into total_loss, then prints the total and the average loss.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [99], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The printed line formats total_loss and total_loss / len directly, so the output includes the tensor repr with grad_fn text such as grad_fn=<AddBackward0>. Changing total_loss to a float via item(), or to a detached tensor, changes that printed text. The retained graph is also negligible, because loss.backward() already freed each batch's saved tensors and only add nodes remain."}]}

