{"step": 2, "unit": "text-search/python/collect_training_data.py::build_dataset", "what_it_does": "Loads the processed query ids, then for each unprocessed query requests its relevant-doc features and a random top-hits sample from Vespa, appends the annotated rows to the training CSV, and appends every query id to the processed-queries file.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [98, 99], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: for every already-processed query the current code opens the processed-queries file in append mode, writes one duplicate line and closes it, so each rerun grows the file and the next run loads the duplicates. Edit: move the `with open(PROCESSED_QUERIES_FILE, 'a')` block (lines 98-99) under the `if int(qid) not in processed_queries:` branch, indented to the level of the `if os.path.isfile` on line 92, so it still runs after the query's to_csv and only for newly processed queries. The printed denominator on line 72 is computed from the file as it exists when the run starts, so for any given starting file the printed output is identical; only the content written to the processed file changes (no duplicate lines), which is the repair itself and the same path is written. Beyond-unit check: grep of the repo shows the processed-queries file is read only by load_processed_queries (line 71) in this module."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [75], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: `int(qid) not in processed_queries` scans a list of P ints for each of Q queries, O(Q x P) comparisons per run. Edit: after line 72 add `processed_set = set(processed_queries)` and change line 75 to test `int(qid) not in processed_set`. Keep the list itself so `len(processed_queries)` on line 72, which counts duplicate lines, is unchanged and the printed denominator matches. Membership results are identical for integer values, and a non-integer qid still raises the same ValueError from int() at the same point. Nothing outside build_dataset is touched."}, {"label": "c", "lines": [92, 97], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Check on the write path: each query's rows are written to OUTPUT_FILE before the same query is recorded in the processed file (line 98). Batching rows and writing periodically would let a crash leave queries recorded as processed with no rows on disk, and the next run would skip them permanently, so the on-disk state after an exception changes. Keeping one open handle across the loop would create OUTPUT_FILE even when no query yields rows, which changes the files written, and would need a flush per query to keep the same on-disk state; no single local edit keeps the contract. The isfile stat is one cheap syscall per query and is not a sound reason to change this."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "text-search/python/collect_training_data.py::get_features", "what_it_does": "POSTs a search request body to the Vespa search URL and returns the root children of the JSON response, or an empty dict after printing a message on a non-200 status.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [38], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: requests.post builds a new Session for every call, so each of the two POSTs per query opens and closes its own TCP connection. Edit: add a module-level `session = requests.Session()` and replace line 38 with `r = session.post(url, json=body)`. Same return value, same printed message on a bad status, and the same requests exception types for connection errors. Beyond-unit check: the new module-level object is read only here, and grep shows get_features is called only from build_dataset (lines 82 and 87). The one behavioural difference is that cookies set by responses persist across calls; this function reads only the status code and JSON body, and nothing in the repo reads cookies."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "text-search/python/convert-to-vespa-format.py::main", "what_it_does": "Reads each document's line from docs.tsv at the offset given for it in test-docs-offset.tsv, keeps lines whose id and field count match, and writes them as JSON lines to documents.jsonl in offset-file order.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [43, 45], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Check on output order: the loop follows the key order of test-docs-offset.tsv (line 43), and each document is written in that order (line 62). Sorting the offsets by position would make the docs.tsv reads sequential, but it reorders the lines in documents.jsonl, so the file content changes. Restoring the original order requires holding all selected documents in memory, which is a different structure and not a local edit."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [62], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: json.dump uses the pure-Python iterencode generator and calls out.write once per fragment of the encoded document, while json.dumps uses the C encoder in one-shot mode and returns the whole string. Edit: replace line 62 `json.dump(doc, out)` with `out.write(json.dumps(doc))`. With default arguments both produce identical text, so the file bytes are the same, and every value is a string so no exception can occur partway through a document. The saving is fewer Python-level calls into the file object and the C encoder replacing the Python loop; buffered writes do not change the syscall count. Nothing outside this unit is touched."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "text-search/python/extract-msmarco.py::extract_documents", "what_it_does": "Writes every document in the lookup table to docs.tsv via getcontent, and writes each docid with its byte offset in docs.tsv to docs-offset.tsv.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [84, 86], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Check on files written: the loop over all docoffset keys (line 84) defines the content of docs.tsv and of docs-offset.tsv, so writing fewer documents changes both files. Beyond-unit check: sample-queries-and-documents.py select_documents (lines 60-62) draws random docids from every key of docs-offset.tsv, so a smaller corpus also changes that script's sampled output. The output contract is the full corpus, so this is rejected."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [85], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: out.tell() on a text-mode TextIOWrapper flushes the write buffer and performs a seek to compute its cookie, once per document, so a run over about 3.2M documents does about that many flush and lseek pairs. Edit: keep `pos = 0` before the loop; for each docid write `offset.write('{0}\\t{1}\\n'...)` using pos, then `s = getcontent(docid, f, docoffset)`, `out.write(s)`, and `pos += len(s.encode('utf8'))`. The file is opened with mode w, utf-8 and no decoder state, so tell() equals the number of bytes written so far and the offset values are identical. This assumes no newline translation, which holds on Linux; on Windows each newline would need one extra byte. Beyond-unit check: docs-offset.tsv is read by sample-queries-and-documents.py (load_document_offsets, lines 37-43), which gets the same values, so it sees no change."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "text-search/python/extract-msmarco.py::load_msmarco_queries", "what_it_does": "Reads the doctrain queries file and returns a dict from query id to the lower-cased query text with non-word characters replaced by spaces; the qrel argument is passed in but never used.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [20, 27], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Check on the waste: the proposed repair skips ids not in qrel (a check before line 26), which saves the regex and lower() only for query lines whose id has no judgements. That saves nothing if every query in the file has judgements, and the data is not in the repo to show otherwise; the queries and qrels come from the same doctrain split. The return dict would also shrink; the only caller is main (line 99), and its result is read only by extract_queries (line 103), which indexes query_strings only for qids in qrel.keys(), so that check would pass. The rejection rests on the waste not being shown to occur."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "text-search/python/sample-queries-and-documents.py::load_queries", "what_it_does": "Reads the queries TSV, normalises each query string with a regex substitution and lower-casing, and returns a dict indexed 0..n-1 of query text and relevant doc id lists.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [29], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Check on output identity: the only producer of queries.tsv in the repo is extract-msmarco.py line 91, and its text already went through the same substitution and lower() (line 26). Removing line 29 is still not output-identical: re.sub followed by lower() can produce a combining mark that the word class does not match. For example U+0130 lower-cases to i plus U+0307, which is not a word character, so the current second pass turns it into a space while the repaired code would keep the combining mark. The contract requires identical written output on every input, so the removal is rejected."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "text-video-search/src/python/app.py::get_predefined_queries", "what_it_does": "Fetches the remote predefined_queries.txt over HTTP on each call and returns its non-empty lines as a list, which is passed to st.selectbox when the checkbox is on.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [33, 36], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Check on return-value freshness: the function returns the remote file as it is at call time, but @st.cache (the decorator on line 23 is used for a model object, where one instance is correct) would return the first fetched list for the life of the process. A later change to the remote file would not be seen, so the value differs from the current function. The object-identity question is not the problem, since the list is only passed as options to st.selectbox (line 44) and never mutated. The repo gives no evidence the remote file is static, so caching is not the same behaviour and is rejected."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "text-video-search/src/python/embedding.py::compute_and_send_video_embeddings", "what_it_does": "For each CLIP model name in turn, builds a VideoFeedDataset and DataLoader over the video folder, and sends each batch of per-frame embedding documents to Vespa, printing a progress line per batch.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [145, 164], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Check on the loop order: the model loop is outer, so each video is decoded once per model. Decoding once would need either (1) a video-outer loop, which changes the order of the printed Model name and Iteration lines and the order of the Vespa update batches, so printed output and the send sequence change; or (2) keeping the decoded frames of all videos across models, so memory grows with the whole corpus for the whole run, and writing them to disk would change the files written. The frames each model receives would be identical in either case, so the problem is order and memory, not frame content. The notebook create-feed-query-text-video-search.ipynb (line 356) also calls this function, so its printed output would change too. No local edit keeps the contract."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "text-video-search/src/python/embedding.py::extract_images", "what_it_does": "Decodes every frame of a video at 1 fps into a list and returns sample_images of that list, an array of number_frames equally spaced frames.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [39, 52], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Check on the return value: an index-first version would compute the indices from len(frames) and return np.array of the selected frames. For number_frames 0 with at least one decoded frame, the current path returns np.array(images)[empty int index], with shape (0, H, W, 3) and dtype uint8, while np.array of an empty selected list gives dtype float64 and shape (0,). That changes the return value on that input. The saving is also small: decoding, the costly part, still runs over every frame, the frames stay in the list until return, and only one stacked copy of a few frames per video is avoided, which is not shown to matter."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "text-video-search/src/python/embedding.py::send_video_embeddings", "what_it_does": "Posts a batch of document updates to Vespa, prints the response codes summary and raises ValueError if any update did not return 200, retried up to three times by tenacity.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [114, 128], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Check on printed output: a targeted retry that re-sends only the failed documents changes what the function prints. The Successfully sent count is computed over the batch passed in, so on a retry it would count only the resent documents, and the printed list of failing responses would also change. The contract requires the same printed output, and the repair changes it on retries. Caller check: send_video_embeddings is called only at line 164 of this file."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "text-video-search/src/python/embedding.py::VideoFeedDataset.__getitem__", "what_it_does": "Decodes a video's frames, converts each to PIL, encodes each frame with the CLIP image encoder one at a time, and returns a list of per-frame Vespa feed documents for the video.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [98, 99], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Check on the return value: stacking the preprocessed frames into one encode_image call would cut the per-call overhead and reuse weights across frames, which is a real saving. But a batched forward pass does not guarantee bit-identical float32 outputs compared with batch size 1, because the matmul kernel and its summation order can depend on the batch size. The returned values are lists of floats sent to Vespa, so the low-order digits of the return value are not guaranteed to match on every input, and the contract requires identical values. Caller check: _from_image_to_vector is called only from line 99 in this file."}]}

