{"step": 2, "unit": "visual-retrieval-colpali/prepare_feed_deploy.py::generate_embeddings", "what_it_does": "Runs the ColPali model over the list of page images through a DataLoader and returns one numpy array of per-page patch embeddings, shape (pages, tokens, 128), with the batch_size parameter meant to group images.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [555, 559], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "The DataLoader is built without batch_size, so it runs at 1 and the batch_size=2 parameter (line 534) is never used; the edit is to pass batch_size=batch_size to the DataLoader call at line 555. Mechanism: model forward calls halve from one per page to one per two pages, cutting per-call overhead and improving GPU batch utilisation. Sole-caller evidence: generate_embeddings is called only at line 574 with the default, so the intended batch is 2. Output contract: collate_fn is processor.process_images, which in the installed colpali_engine (utils/colpali_processing_utils.py) uses the same prompt for every image with padding='longest' and a fixed max_length, so sequence length does not depend on batch composition and no new padding appears; the ColPali forward applies the attention mask per sample. Residual differences: batched matmuls may change float rounding in the last bits, dtype and shape are unchanged, and the tqdm iteration count changes."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [561, 568], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The premise is wrong: np.concatenate calls Tensor.__array__ once per unbound view and copies each view in bulk, not element by element. The same bytes are copied whether or not the batch is unbound; the only removed work is one Python-level view and conversion call per image, microseconds against a full ColPali forward per image, so the repair (torch.cat then one numpy call) yields no measurable energy saving."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "visual-retrieval-colpali/prepare_feed_deploy.py::float_to_binary_embedding", "what_it_does": "Converts a dict of float embedding rows into a dict of int8 lists holding the sign bits packed eight per byte, one list per key.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [584, 592], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "The per-row loop runs np.array, np.where, packbits, astype and tolist for each of about 1030 rows per page. Edit: stack the rows into one (K, D) array, compare with 0 once, call np.packbits(axis=1), astype(np.int8), and build the result as zip of keys with row.tolist(); keep the original loop as a fallback when the dict is empty or row shapes differ, so ragged or empty inputs behave as before. Mechanism: about 4 numpy calls per row (about 4000 per page) become a few whole-array calls; the per-row tolist to Python ints remains. Byte identity: packbits along axis 1 equals per-row packbits on the flattened row, the zero padding of the last byte is the same, and int8 wrap and tolist give the same ints. Sole-caller evidence: the only call is line 611 in this file; the same-named method in src/backend/vespa_app.py is a separate class method that this function does not call and is not changed."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "visual-retrieval-colpali/prepare_feed_deploy.py::generate_queries", "what_it_does": "Sends one page image and the query prompt to Gemini and returns the parsed JSON dict of queries, or a dict of six empty strings on any error.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [457, 459], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The check that decided it: caching or skipping requests needs a new reader of output/pdf_pages.json, which is written at lines 517-520 and whose only reader is commented out at lines 522-525; there is no reader in the repo and no output directory is present. Even with a reader, reusing saved queries replaces fresh LLM output with stale output from an earlier run, and the all-empty fallback from failed requests would be cached as if valid, so the produced data changes. That edit reaches the loop at 457-459 and the writer at 517-520, both beyond this unit. Making generate_queries concurrent would change it to async and its return type, which breaks its caller at line 459 and the commented-out async code, and cannot be done inside this unit."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "visual-retrieval-colpali/prepare_feed_deploy.py::download_pdf", "what_it_does": "Downloads one PDF from a URL with up to max_attempts tries, derives a filesystem-safe filename from Content-Disposition or the URL, writes it under pdfs/, and returns the path or None.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [265, 268], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Reachable infinite loop: when safe_filename is '_' (URL basename '_', or Content-Disposition filename of '/' or of a backslash), the branch prints and hits continue before attempt += 1, so the while condition never changes and the URL is re-fetched and its body re-read forever with no sleep. Edit: place attempt += 1 before continue at line 268 so the invalid branch counts toward max_attempts and the function returns None. Contract: this branch never reached a write or a return before, so the files written and return values are unchanged on every input that terminated; the only printed-output change is that the infinite stream of 'Invalid filename' lines becomes three. Sole-caller evidence: download_pdf is called only at line 296 in download_pdfs, which filters falsy paths. Mechanism: stops an unbounded series of HTTP GETs and full-body downloads."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "visual-retrieval-colpali/refeed_from_hf.py::pil_to_base64", "what_it_does": "Encodes a PIL image to JPEG at default quality and returns the base64 text of those bytes.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [24, 30], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The check that decided it: inside this function the input is already a decoded PIL image, so the original stored bytes are not available here. The only edit in this unit that skips the re-encode would return base64 of different bytes than today's re-encoded JPEG, changing the return value for every caller. Its only caller is process_item_for_vespa (lines 36-37), so avoiding the decode requires changing that caller and the module-level load_dataset cast at line 15, which is outside this unit."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "visual-retrieval-colpali/refeed_from_hf.py::process_item_for_vespa", "what_it_does": "Maps one dataset row to a Vespa document dict, with blur and full images as base64 JPEG strings and the embedding dict rebuilt from embedding.N columns.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [36, 37], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The check that decided it: passing raw stored bytes (datasets Image with decode=False, set at module-level line 15 outside this unit) changes the returned blur_image and full_image strings for every page, because base64 of the original JPEG bytes differs from base64 of today's re-encoded JPEG. The return value contract is therefore broken. Sole-caller evidence (only ds.map at line 67) was verified, but it does not restore the return value, and the repair also needs the edit at line 15, outside this unit."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "visual-retrieval-colpali/vespa_feed_to_hf_dataset.py::save_image", "what_it_does": "Base64-decodes a JPEG string, opens it with PIL and saves it to the given filename, which re-encodes it as JPEG at default quality.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [21, 24], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The check that decided it: writing the decoded bytes directly would remove one decode and one lossy re-encode, but it changes the bytes of every file written to hf_dataset/images, since the current output is a second JPEG generation at quality 75 and the raw write keeps the feed bytes. The same-files-written contract is therefore not met. The dataset is also pushed to the Hub at line 42, so the published bytes would change as well."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "visual-retrieval-colpali/tests/huggingface_smoke.py::main", "what_it_does": "Imports the Space app with a test client, checks that three routes return 200, and when --load-model is set asserts that the LoRA-B adapter weights are non-zero and that query embeddings are finite and 2-D with width 128.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [66, 70], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Edit: wrap the lora_b check at lines 68-70 in with torch.no_grad(), or use p.detach().abs().sum(), so the abs, sum and comparison ops are not recorded in an autograd graph; loaded model parameters require grad by default, so each of the LoRA-B checks currently allocates graph nodes and keeps saved tensors alive. Mechanism: no autograd bookkeeping or graph memory per LoRA-B parameter. The assertion result, AssertionError message and printed output are unchanged, and the lora_b list and model are local to main (sole-caller evidence: the model is used only in this function). The saving is small because this is a one-shot check on small tensors."}]}

