{"step": 2, "unit": "feed_to_vespa.py::update_endpoint", "what_it_does": "Reads the indexed docids from Vespa, optionally deletes all of them, computes the feed docid set from the feed files, deletes indexed docs missing from the feed, and feeds each file through the vespa CLI, printing progress headers and counts.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [152, 158, 172, 177], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Waste applies when do_index_removal_before_feed is true: line 158 DELETEs every id in docids_in_index, then line 177 DELETEs docids_to_remove, a subset of that same set with no PUT in between. Repair: guard the line 177 call with `if not do_remove_index:` and keep the 'To Remove' prints and the count print, so stdout is unchanged. Remote state is unchanged: the document is already gone, and a repeat DELETE of a missing id leaves the index as it was. In this file vespa_delete uses a requests session with Retry, so a connection failure or exhausted retries raises before the second loop is reached, and any other response returns a JSON body that a repeat would reproduce. Mechanism: one fewer HTTP round trip per redundant id. All three shipped _config files set do_index_removal_before_feed to false, so the saving applies only when removal is enabled. Sole-caller evidence: update_endpoint has one caller, main at line 201, and the guard reads only the local do_remove_index."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [153], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Checked the printed output: line 154 prints the count of docids_in_index unconditionally, so skipping the visit changes stdout even when neither removal nor feed is requested. Making only the print conditional also changes stdout, and the set is needed for the difference in the feed branch. No repair keeps the printed contract."}, {"label": "c", "lines": [164, 166], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Waste applies: the shipped _config.yml lists at least seven feed_endpoints that all name vespaapps_index.json, so get_feed_docids re-opens, json-loads and rebuilds the same set once per endpoint. Repair: add an optional feed_cache=None parameter to update_endpoint; before line 166 look up (index, namespace, doc_type) in the cache and call get_feed_docids only on a miss, and when feed_cache is None call it directly. In main create one dict before the loop at line 200 and pass it at line 201. Output is unchanged because get_feed_docids prints and writes nothing and the cached set is only read by union; a failing read stores nothing, so the exception path is the same. Mechanism: all but one of the open, json.load and set-building passes are skipped. Sole-caller evidence: update_endpoint has one caller in the repo (main, line 201), and .github/workflows/feed.yml runs this script once per config file, so the cache is scoped to one config."}, {"label": "d", "lines": [166], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Waste applies: line 166 rebinds docids_in_feed to a new set that copies the accumulated set plus the new docids, once per index file. Repair: `docids_in_feed.update(get_feed_docids(index, namespace, doc_type))`. docids_in_feed is the fresh set() from line 162 and is used only for len() and difference(), and get_feed_docids returns a set for the valid doc types. For an unknown doc type it returns None, and union and update both raise TypeError with the same message. Mechanism: one full copy of the set is removed per index file, which is one per endpoint in the shipped configs. The saving is small, so this is a minor accepted item."}, {"label": "e", "lines": [175, 176], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The per-document 'To Remove:' print at line 176 is stdout that the workflow logs. Removing it or merging it into one summary print changes printed output, which the contract forbids, so no repair is allowed."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "feed_to_vespa.py::vespa_remove", "what_it_does": "Issues one HTTP DELETE per document id through vespa_delete and discards every response.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [64, 68], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The parse lives in vespa_delete at line 52 (session.delete(url).json()), and vespa_delete has one caller in this file (line 68). Dropping .json() would stop a non-JSON response body (for example an HTML proxy error that Retry does not cover) from raising JSONDecodeError, which changes the exception type and the stop-the-run behaviour. The edit would also reach into vespa_delete, which is outside this unit. Batching the DELETEs needs a different transport, such as the vespa feed CLI with remove operations, which changes the remote calls and error handling. No in-unit repair keeps the contract, so it is rejected."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "incremental-search/search-as-you-type/feed_to_vespa.py::vespa_post", "what_it_does": "Sends one document to the Vespa document API by spawning a curl subprocess and returns curl's stdout bytes.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [48, 58], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The repair replaces the curl Popen call in call() with a requests POST, which changes the transport and error paths. call() never checks curl's exit code, and curl's stderr goes straight to the console, so a connection failure today prints curl's message and returns empty bytes. A requests session would raise instead, and the file has no session or cert setup to match. The exit-code and error outcomes are not identical, so the rule rejects the transport change."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "incremental-search/search-as-you-type/feed_to_vespa.py::vespa_feed", "what_it_does": "Reads a feed JSON file, POSTs each document with vespa_post, and prints each response body.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [83, 86], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Batching the posts would either need one curl process for many documents or a changed vespa_post contract. Line 86 prints each vespa_post result, so the stdout shape would change from one block per document to a different form, which the contract forbids. The double parse mentioned in the step-1 text is not on lines 83 or 86; removing it needs get_feed_docids in update_endpoint to change, which is another unit. Rejected on the printed-output contract and on the transport change."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "incremental-search/search-as-you-type/feed_to_vespa.py::vespa_remove", "what_it_does": "Prints a Removing line and issues one curl DELETE subprocess per document id.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [75, 80], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The repair replaces the curl DELETE subprocess in vespa_delete with a requests call, which changes the transport and the error path. call() ignores curl's exit code and lets curl's stderr through, so a connection failure is silent apart from curl's message, whereas requests would raise and stop the run. The exit-code and error-path outcomes are not identical, so the transport change is rejected. The same reasoning applies to label a of vespa_post in this file."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "incremental-search/search-as-you-type/feed_to_vespa.py::update_endpoint", "what_it_does": "Reads indexed docids via a visit, optionally deletes them all, deletes indexed docs missing from the feed, then feeds each index file through vespa_feed while printing progress.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [139, 142, 155, 161], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Skipping the second vespa_remove at line 160 when do_remove_index is set has two observable effects. First, vespa_remove prints 'Removing: <id>' at line 79 for every id, so stdout changes. Second, this file's vespa_delete ignores curl's exit code, so a first DELETE that failed transiently leaves the document in the index, and the second DELETE is the only retry. Skipping it changes the remote state in that case. The root file's requests-based version raises instead, which is why the same edit is not equally safe here. Rejected on both the printed-output contract and the remote-state contract."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [149], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The second parse is in vespa_feed at line 84 (for doc in get_docs(feed)), not in this function. Removing it needs vespa_feed to accept already-parsed data and get_feed_docids to return that data, so the repair edits two other functions and changes their contracts. That is a multi-unit refactor, not a repair to this unit, even though vespa_feed has one caller at line 167."}, {"label": "c", "lines": [149, 166], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Waste applies: line 149 rebinds docids_in_feed to a copy of the accumulated set on each index file. Repair: `docids_in_feed.update(get_feed_docids(index, namespace))`. In this file get_feed_docids always returns a set, and docids_in_feed is the fresh set() from line 145, used only for len() and difference(), so the in-place update gives the same value. Mechanism: one full copy of the set is removed per index file. Line 166 is the print_header for feeding and contains no union, so the edit is only at line 149. Sole-caller evidence: every read and write involved is local to update_endpoint."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "feed-split.py::split_text", "what_it_does": "Splits the markdown of a soup into (id, header, text) sections at each heading line, appending each non-heading line to the current section's text.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [97], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Waste applies: line 97 text = text + newline + line copies the whole accumulated section on every line, so a section of k lines costs time proportional to k times its length. Repair: keep a list buf of lines; on a heading test `if buf:` and append (id, header, ''.join of a newline character plus each line in buf), then reset buf to an empty list; use the same join at the final flush at line 99. The joined text is byte-identical: the original text is exactly the concatenation of a newline plus each line in order, it is non-empty exactly when at least one line was added (so `if buf` matches `if text`), and it is the empty string otherwise, as the empty join is. Mechanism: the growing string is no longer copied per line, so the cost is linear. Sole-caller evidence: split_text has one caller, main at line 157, and the returned list shape is unchanged."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "feed-split.py::table_header_row", "what_it_does": "Returns the first tr inside the thead of the table that contains a row, or None when the table has no thead.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [115, 126, 135], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Hoisting the header lookup out of the per-row call needs state across calls, either a cache keyed by table or changes to move_linkable_item_to_single_entity and split_tables. Both are outside this unit, and a module-level cache keyed by object id is unsafe because ids get reused. The in-unit alternative, find('thead', recursive=False), changes results: today find descends into nested tables, so a table without a direct thead but with a thead in a nested table returns the nested header, and the recursive=False version would return None. Output would change on that input, so the repair is rejected."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "feed-split.py::xml_fixup", "what_it_does": "Escapes the text of each Jekyll xml highlight block by applying text.replace of the escaped block across the whole document.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [36, 41], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The loop calls text.replace on the whole document, so it escapes every occurrence of each matched string, including occurrences outside its block and occurrences created by earlier replacements. A per-block re.sub, or processing only unique matches, changes the output in two cases: when the match text also occurs outside its block, and when the escaped form contains the original text (for example '&amp;' escapes to '&amp;amp;'), so a duplicate block would be escaped again by the current code. The output is not preserved on every input, so the repair is rejected."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "feed-split.py::main", "what_it_does": "Reads the docs JSON, converts each doc's HTML to sections with html5lib and split_text, builds paragraph documents with question expansions, and writes paragraph_index.json.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [155], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Switching the parser from html5lib to lxml or html.parser changes the tree that split_tables relies on. Line 117 says tbody is implicit and always there, which holds for html5lib; html.parser does not insert a tbody, so table.find('tbody') returns None and line 118 raises AttributeError. Malformed markup also yields a different tree and different paragraphs. The output and the exception path both change, so the semantic risk is confirmed and the repair is rejected."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "reverse-image-search/script/feed.py::run", "what_it_does": "For each batch of ImageNet images, saves any missing JPEGs, computes DINOv2 CLS embeddings from the re-decoded JPEG files, and feeds the records to Vespa with feed_iterable, logging progress per batch.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [152, 153], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The redundant work is real, but the repair changes the embedding input. The in-memory PIL image is the pre-save image, while the re-decoded file is the JPEG encoded at quality 88 after the convert and any resize, which is lossy, so an embedding computed from the in-memory image differs from the current one. On the refeed path for files already on disk there is no in-memory image at all, so that branch still needs the decode. read_bytes must stay because full_image needs the bytes, so only the decode could be removed, and that is the step that changes the embedding. Rejected under the embedding-change rule."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [134, 141], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Moving the exists check above img = ex['image'] at line 136 would avoid decoding skipped images, but it changes two observable things. A decode error for an image already on disk would no longer raise, which changes the exception path of run. Also, the isinstance check at line 137 runs before the skip counter today, so a non-PIL value for an existing file would move from a silent drop into total_skip, changing the progress and done log records at lines 188 and 191. Rejected on the exception and log contract."}, {"label": "c", "lines": [182, 187], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Calling feed_iterable once for all batches would change the cadence of the progress log at lines 188-189 and the done counters, and it would require restructuring the embed-then-feed interleaving, so the log records and failure reporting order change. The stated mechanism, per-call worker-pool and connection setup inside pyvespa, was not checked against the pyvespa source, so no saving is shown. Rejected on the log-record contract and the unverified mechanism."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "hypencoder/encode_query.py::main", "what_it_does": "Tokenizes the query text and writes a Vespa query body as JSON to stdout, with padded token ids and attention mask as lists of floats.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [70, 72], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Waste applies: lines 70 and 72 build np.array of Python int lists with dtype float32 and call tolist(), which returns the same float values as converting each element with float(). Repair: delete `import numpy as np` at line 30 and write body['input.query(input_ids)'] = [[float(i) for i in enc.ids]] and body['input.query(attention_mask)'] = [[float(m) for m in enc.attention_mask]]. The output is identical: float32 represents every integer up to 2 to the 24th power exactly, so the round trip gives the same Python floats and json.dump writes the same text, provided the vocabulary ids stay below that bound; I did not read tokenizer.json to confirm the vocabulary size. Mechanism: numpy is no longer imported on each CLI invocation, and two temporary arrays are not built per query. The numpy entry in hypencoder/requirements.txt is left in place because hypencoder/model2onnx.py still imports numpy. Sole-caller evidence: the only uses of np are at lines 70 and 72 in this file."}]}

