{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/agentic-streamlit-chatbot/advanced_app/vespa_shopping_assistant/langgraph_modules/subgraph.py::EvaluateResults", "what_it_does": "Sends the search results and conversation to gpt-4o-mini to select up to five relevant results, then writes the parsed FinalResults back into state SearchEngineResults and prints it.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [197], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: each call builds a new ChatOpenAI, which creates its own OpenAI HTTP client and connection pool, so every LLM call repeats TCP and TLS setup instead of reusing a connection. Edit: add a module-level cache (model = None and a helper _get_chat_model that builds ChatOpenAI(model='gpt-4o-mini', temperature=0) on first call and returns the cached instance), then replace the constructor on line 197 with model = _get_chat_model(). The build must be lazy, not at import, because main_graph.py imports subgraph at its line 4 before it sets OPENAI_API_KEY at lines 14-15, so an import-time constructor would fail when the variable is unset. Sole-reader evidence: the node is referenced only at subgraph.py lines 235-240, the compiled graph is used only by main_graph.py and streamlit_app.py, and the cached model holds no per-call state. Caveat: langchain_openai is not installed in the sandbox venv, so the per-instance client cost rests on the library design and was not measured."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/agentic-streamlit-chatbot/advanced_app/vespa_shopping_assistant/langgraph_modules/subgraph.py::subgraph_EvaluateCategory", "what_it_does": "Asks gpt-4o-mini to classify the user query into product categories and writes Categories and ClarifyingQuestion into state.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [99], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Same edit as unit subgraph_EvaluateResults (label a): replace the ChatOpenAI constructor on line 99 with the shared lazily cached _get_chat_model() helper. Same mechanism (no per-call HTTP client or connection reuse lost today), same lazy-init reason (main_graph.py line 4 imports subgraph before OPENAI_API_KEY is set on lines 14-15), and same sole-reader evidence (node wired only at subgraph.py lines 235-240). Same caveat: langchain_openai is not installed in the sandbox venv, so the cost was not measured."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/agentic-streamlit-chatbot/advanced_app/vespa_shopping_assistant/langgraph_modules/subgraph.py::subgraph_EvaluateFilters", "what_it_does": "Asks gpt-4o-mini to extract price and rating filters from the conversation and writes them to state Filters, appending 'quantity > 0' if absent.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [149], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Same edit as unit subgraph_EvaluateResults (label a): replace the ChatOpenAI constructor on line 149 with the shared lazily cached _get_chat_model() helper. Same mechanism, same lazy-init reason, same sole-reader evidence (node wired only at subgraph.py lines 235-240). Same caveat: langchain_openai is not installed in the sandbox venv, so the cost was not measured."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/agentic-streamlit-chatbot/advanced_app/vespa_shopping_assistant/langgraph_modules/subgraph.py::subgraph_GenerateKeywords", "what_it_does": "Asks gpt-4o-mini to generate Vespa search keywords from the conversation and writes them to state SearchEngineQuery.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [48], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Same edit as unit subgraph_EvaluateResults (label a): replace the ChatOpenAI constructor on line 48 with the shared lazily cached _get_chat_model() helper. Same mechanism, same lazy-init reason, same sole-reader evidence (node wired only at subgraph.py lines 235-240). Same caveat: langchain_openai is not installed in the sandbox venv, so the cost was not measured."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/agentic-streamlit-chatbot/advanced_app/vespa_shopping_assistant/vespa_module/vespa_retriever.py::VespaRetriever", "what_it_does": "Builds a YQL query with category, filter and hybrid-ranking conditions, runs it on Vespa, and writes the hit fields to state SearchEngineResults.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [67], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Checked pyvespa 1.2.9 at venv site-packages/vespa/application.py: Vespa.__init__ (about lines 285-330) only stores url, cert, key and headers and does no network or certificate I/O, so hoisting the Vespa object saves essentially nothing. The real per-call cost is inside Vespa.query, which opens a new VespaSync (pool of 1) on every call (about line 655), and hoisting the client does not change that."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/agentic-streamlit-chatbot/simple_app/streamlit_vespa_app.py::VespaRetriever", "what_it_does": "Runs a hybrid Vespa query for the user text inside a syncio session and returns the hit fields as an indented JSON string for the LLM tool call.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [50, 54], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Same pyvespa check: Vespa(...) at line 50 does no I/O, so hoisting it saves nothing. The expensive part is the syncio session opened per call at line 54, and reusing it needs a session that outlives the call. Streamlit re-executes this whole module on every interaction, so a module-level session is rebuilt on each rerun; keeping one across reruns needs st.cache_resource, which is state outside this function and was not verified to be safe."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/book-search/frontend/app.py::feed_catalog", "what_it_does": "When the index is empty, reads every line of data/documents.jsonl and feeds each document to Vespa with its own feed_data_point request, ignoring responses.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [18, 25], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The per-document cost is real: VespaSync.feed_data_point opens a new session per call (application.py about line 780). But the batch repair, feed_iterable with no callback, changes error behavior: in _handle_result_callback (application.py about lines 912-940) exceptions and non-2xx results become status-599 responses and are dropped silently, while today VespaSync.feed_data_point calls raise_for_status (application.py line 1817) so a rejected document raises VespaError out of feed_catalog. A callback cannot re-raise (user callback exceptions are printed and swallowed), and collecting errors to raise later changes how many documents are fed before the failure. The exception contract changes, so REJECT."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/ecommerce-user-preferences/webapp/app.py::chat", "what_it_does": "Builds the OpenAI request from the system prompt, facet filter context and session history, calls the model, stores preferences in the session, and queries Vespa for results and facets.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [435], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "load_system_prompt() is called only at line 435 (grep verified), which sits before the try block that starts at line 451, so an unreadable file currently raises out of chat(). Hoisting the read to module import would make app.py fail at import instead, and would stop picking up edits to system_prompt.txt made while the server runs. The saving is one small page-cached file read per chat request, negligible next to the LLM call, so the change trades a real behavior for almost nothing."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [445], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The suspected waste is the print of the full system prompt. Removing it changes printed output, which the contract forbids, so no repair that keeps the output identical exists."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/ecommerce-user-preferences/webapp/app.py::get_car_image", "what_it_does": "Returns a cached image URL for a make and model, otherwise fetches one from Wikipedia and caches it, returning 404 when none is found.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [571, 577], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Caching the negative result would write a None entry into IMAGE_CACHE, and that dict has other readers that break: get_car_image itself at lines 563-565 would return a 200 with image_url null instead of the 404, and get_wikipedia_image at lines 200-205 returns any cached entry sharing the make prefix, so a None entry would block the similar-make fallback. The exception path in get_wikipedia_image (line 214) also returns None for transient timeouts, so caching negatives would make a transient failure permanent until restart. Checked by grep that IMAGE_CACHE is read only at lines 69, 200-205, 563-565 of app.py."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/ecommerce-user-preferences/webapp/app.py::get_wikipedia_image", "what_it_does": "Searches Wikipedia for a make and model, lists the page images, and returns the first suitable image URL, caching hits in IMAGE_CACHE and falling back to a similar make.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [137, 152, 170, 185], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: the fallback loop (lines 167-196) re-requests imageinfo for titles the first keyword loop (lines 133-163) already requested. The first loop falls through only when no title returned a URL, so each repeated title is known to yield no URL, and the second loop would get the same empty answer. Edit: keep a set of titles queried in the first loop and skip any title in that set in the second loop. Return values and prints are unchanged, and each skipped request is a Wikipedia round trip saved. Sole-caller evidence: get_wikipedia_image is called only from get_car_image at line 569 (grep), and the edit uses only locals inside the function."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [137, 152, 170, 185], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Batching titles into one imageinfo request changes what is fetched and needs the response pages mapped back to requested titles. The code takes the first page with a URL per title, and MediaWiki keys pages by pageid and may normalize or redirect titles; that mapping cannot be verified offline in this sandbox, so order-preserving equivalence is not shown. The first keyword title also succeeds on the common path, so batching would fetch extra titles there."}, {"label": "c", "lines": [86, 88], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: requests.get creates and closes a new Session per call, so each Wikipedia request pays a new TCP and TLS setup. Edit: create one requests.Session at the top of get_wikipedia_image (inside its try) and call session.get in place of requests.get for every search and imageinfo request in the function, including the retry at lines 96-98. Lines 86 and 88 alone would save nothing, since they are one request, so the session has to cover the later calls in the same invocation. Same headers, params and timeouts, same exceptions, so the Timeout branch and return values are unchanged. Cookies from Wikipedia can persist within one call, which does not affect the anonymous API results."}, {"label": "d", "lines": [207, 214], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Caching the None result from line 207 or line 214 has the same readers as get_car_image label a: line 69-71 returns the cached value directly, lines 200-205 pick any cached entry with the make prefix so a None entry would block the similar-make fallback, and get_car_image line 563-565 would return a 200 with null. Caching the exception path also makes a transient timeout permanent until restart."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/ecommerce-user-preferences/webapp/app.py::query_vespa", "what_it_does": "Turns preferences and facet filters into a Vespa ranking query, prints it, posts it with mTLS, and returns the JSON results with car_id added, or None on error.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [279], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The suspected waste is the json.dumps(query, indent=2) printed on each query. Removing or changing the print changes printed output, which the contract forbids, so no repair that keeps the output identical exists."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [283, 287], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The only repair that saves a handshake across queries is a module-level requests.Session shared by all request handlers. app.run defaults to threaded=True (verified in venv flask/app.py line 655), so concurrent requests would share one Session and its cookie jar, and requests does not document Session as thread-safe. A per-call Session saves nothing for a single POST, so this is not a safe repair."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/ecommerce-user-preferences/webapp/app.py::query_vespa_facets", "what_it_does": "Builds a grouped facet query for make, transmission and fuel type (plus model when one make is selected), prints it, posts it with mTLS, and returns the JSON or None.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [387], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Same as query_vespa label a: the print of json.dumps(query, indent=2) is printed output, and the contract forbids changing it."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [391, 395], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Same as query_vespa label b: a shared module-level Session would be used by threaded Flask request handlers (app.run threaded=True), and per-call Sessions save nothing for one POST."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/embedder-auto-training-evaluation/training-evaluation/evaluate.py::search", "what_it_does": "Posts a nearest-neighbour query for one query text, retrying once on any exception, and appends ranked non-self hits to the global responses list.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [22, 25], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The second session.post is a deliberate retry after the Retry adapter (total=20, connect=20) gives up. Removing the bare except changes outcomes: a failure that the second attempt would clear would now raise and abort the whole run, changing the written run file. Narrowing the except still changes which failures get a second attempt, so it is not a pure saving."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/embedder-auto-training-evaluation/training-evaluation/positives-negatives.py::search", "what_it_does": "Posts a nearest-neighbour query for one query text, retrying once on any exception, and appends a positives and negatives record to the global train_data list.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [22, 25], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Same as evaluate.py search label a: the second post is a deliberate retry after the Retry adapter gives up, and removing or narrowing the bare except changes which failures are retried and whether the run completes."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/google-cloud/cloud-functions/python/main.py::get_blobs", "what_it_does": "Creates a new storage.Client on each call and returns the lazy blob iterator for a bucket, used by listbucket, listfiles and getlogs.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [87, 90], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: storage.Client() on every request re-runs default credential discovery (which can query the metadata server on Cloud Functions) and builds a new authorized HTTP session, so every request repeats that setup. Edit: add a module-level client variable and a helper that creates storage.Client() on first use and returns the cached client, and make get_blobs call it instead of constructing a client. The build stays lazy so failures still raise inside get_blobs at call time, and a failed build is not cached. Sole-caller evidence: grep shows get_blobs is called only at main.py lines 29, 43 and 58 (listbucket, listfiles, getlogs), and each consumes the returned iterator within the same request; the return value is still the same lazy iterator."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/in-context-learning/categorize_group.py::inference", "what_it_does": "Posts a fixed bm25 grouped query to local Vespa for one banking77 text and returns the parsed (text, relevance, label) examples.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [211], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: each requests.post call opens and closes a new TCP connection to localhost:8080, once per row over up to 1000 rows. Edit: create a module-level requests.Session() next to the client setup and call session.post(...) on line 211 instead of requests.post. Same request body, same response handling, same printed output. Sole-caller evidence: grep shows inference is called only at line 247 in the module-level loop, which is single-threaded, so sharing one Session is safe. The saving is small (localhost connection setup) but it is a real per-request cost removed."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/model-exporting/export_hf_model_from_hf.py::export_model", "what_it_does": "Exports an embedding model to ONNX, saves the tokenizer to the output directory, then deletes every saved tokenizer file except tokenizer.json, printing each deletion.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [42, 49], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The deletion loop prints one line per removed file (line 48). A repair that avoids writing those files would drop the printed lines and change the set of files written, both forbidden by the contract. The saving (a few small tokenizer writes next to an ONNX export) is also negligible."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/model-exporting/export_model_from_hf.py::export_model", "what_it_does": "Exports an embedding model to ONNX, saves the tokenizer to the output directory, then deletes every saved tokenizer file except vocab.txt, printing each deletion.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [42, 48], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Same as export_hf_model_from_hf label a: the deletion loop prints one line per removed file (line 47), and avoiding the writes would change printed output and the set of files written, which the contract forbids."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/workshop-ecommerce/preprocess.py::process_orders", "what_it_does": "Groups order_products by order, merges the aggregated product lists onto the filtered orders for each eval set, and writes orders_<eval_set>.jsonl with at most limit documents in sample mode.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [234], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Removing the orders.csv re-read needs process_users and process_orders to share one frame, which means a new parameter threaded through main() (lines 397-412). Moving the single read into main before process_users would make a missing orders.csv fail before the 'Processing users...' line prints, changing printed output on that error path. Inside process_orders alone the read cannot be removed, so there is no single-unit edit."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [236, 272], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: in sample mode the full order_products groupby with a Python list lambda runs over all eval-set rows (about 1.4M train rows) when only limit orders are written. Edit: after the len==0 check and the file read, when not full, keep df_order_filtered.head(limit) and filter df_order_products to rows whose order_id is in that subset before the sort and groupby. The left merge preserves left order and the aggregated right side is unique by order_id, so the first limit merged rows are identical. The printed total min(limit, len(...)) is unchanged, and the len==0 skip still uses the full filter. Sole-caller evidence: process_orders is called only from main() at line 412, and nothing else reads its intermediate frames."}, {"label": "c", "lines": [290, 305], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: iterrows materializes the whole frame as an object array and builds one pandas Series per row, which is the costly path in full mode (sample mode still converts the whole frame once). Edit: replace enumerate(df_orders_vespa.iterrows()) with enumerate(df_orders_vespa.itertuples(index=False)) and read fields as attributes (order.order_id, order.user_id, order.order_dow, order.order_hour_of_day, order.days_since_prior_order, order.product_ids). The int, float, pd.notna conversions and the f-string ids produce the same text, so the written file is identical. Only the full-mode path gains much, since sample mode stops after limit rows."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/workshop-ecommerce/preprocess.py::process_products", "what_it_does": "Reads products, aisles and departments CSVs, left-merges names onto products, and writes products.jsonl with at most limit documents in sample mode.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [139, 146, 149, 152], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The repair would read only the first limit product rows and merge those, which is output-identical only if aisles.aisle_id and departments.department_id are unique (a duplicate key would multiply merged rows and change which documents are written). The script does not check this, and the dataset CSVs are not in the repo (workshop-ecommerce/dataset holds only jsonl outputs), so equivalence on every input is not shown. The saving (about 50k rows read and merged once) is also small."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/workshop-ecommerce/preprocess.py::process_users", "what_it_does": "Reads orders and the train order_products CSV, merges user_id onto each purchase, groups by user, and writes users.jsonl with a per-user profile of product counts and a purchase array, at most limit users in sample mode.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [175], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Same as process_orders label a: sharing the orders.csv read needs a parameter threaded through main(), and moving the read earlier would make a missing orders.csv fail before the 'Processing users...' print, changing printed output on that path. The read cannot be removed from process_users alone."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [181, 198], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Restricting the merge to the first limit users needs the set of those users, which requires the same full merge against orders. Making that restriction exact depends on orders.order_id being unique (the right side of the merge at lines 181-185), which the script does not enforce and which cannot be verified because the CSV is not in the repo, so equivalence on every input is not shown."}, {"label": "c", "lines": [202, 209], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: iterrows builds one pandas Series per purchase row in every group, which dominates in full mode (sample mode stops after limit groups). Edit: replace user_purchases_df.iterrows() with itertuples(index=False) and read order_dow, order_hour_of_day, order_id and product_id as attributes. The int conversions give the same values, so the purchase_struct dicts and the written users.jsonl are identical. The purchase order is unchanged because itertuples preserves row order."}, {"label": "d", "lines": [192, 198], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "value_counts() (line 195) orders ties in pandas' own sort order, and user_profile keys are inserted in that order and written by json.dumps (line 219). A groupby-based recount, which would replace the per-group DataFrame, can change tie order and so the bytes of users.jsonl. The per-group DataFrame iteration is also the groupby loop itself, so the output contract cannot be kept with a faster equivalent."}]}

