{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/workshop-ecommerce/preprocess.py::process_orders", "what_it_does": "Groups order_products by order, merges the aggregated product lists onto the filtered orders for each eval set, and writes orders_<eval_set>.jsonl with at most limit documents in sample mode.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [234], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Removing the orders.csv re-read needs process_users and process_orders to share one frame, which means a new parameter threaded through main() (lines 397-412). Moving the single read into main before process_users would make a missing orders.csv fail before the 'Processing users...' line prints, changing printed output on that error path. Inside process_orders alone the read cannot be removed, so there is no single-unit edit."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [236, 272], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: in sample mode the full order_products groupby with a Python list lambda runs over all eval-set rows (about 1.4M train rows) when only limit orders are written. Edit: after the len==0 check and the file read, when not full, keep df_order_filtered.head(limit) and filter df_order_products to rows whose order_id is in that subset before the sort and groupby. The left merge preserves left order and the aggregated right side is unique by order_id, so the first limit merged rows are identical. The printed total min(limit, len(...)) is unchanged, and the len==0 skip still uses the full filter. Sole-caller evidence: process_orders is called only from main() at line 412, and nothing else reads its intermediate frames."}, {"label": "c", "lines": [290, 305], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Mechanism: iterrows materializes the whole frame as an object array and builds one pandas Series per row, which is the costly path in full mode (sample mode still converts the whole frame once). Edit: replace enumerate(df_orders_vespa.iterrows()) with enumerate(df_orders_vespa.itertuples(index=False)) and read fields as attributes