{"step": 2, "unit": "reverse-image-search/script/feed.py::run", "what_it_does": "For each batch of ImageNet images, saves missing JPEGs, computes DINOv2 CLS embeddings from the re-decoded files and feeds the records with feed_iterable, logging progress.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [152, 153], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "The embedding would be computed from the pre-save in-memory image instead of the quality-88 re-decoded JPEG, so the vectors change. The refeed path has no in-memory image at all."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [134, 141], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Moving the exists check above ex['image'] changes the exception path for a bad existing file and moves some skips into total_skip, changing the logged progress records."}, {"label": "c", "lines": [182, 187], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "One feed_iterable call changes the cadence of the per-batch progress records and the failure-reporting order. The pyvespa setup cost was not verified."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "hypencoder/encode_query.py::main", "what_it_does": "Tokenizes the query and writes a Vespa query body as JSON to stdout, with token ids and attention mask as float lists.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [70, 72], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "np.array(..., dtype=float32).tolist() gives the same floats as float(i) per element for integers below 2^24, and json.dump writes identical text. Repair: drop numpy from this function and build the float lists directly. Not confirmed here that the vocabulary stays below 2^24; numpy stays in requirements because model2onnx.py uses it."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "model-inference/src/main/application/models/pytorch_pairwise_ranker.py::PairwiseRankerModel.forward", "what_it_does": "Builds a graph that concatenates the query with each of two documents, passes each pair through a shared linear and sigmoid, and returns a score; reached only through torch.onnx.export.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [16, 19], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Splitting the linear into query and document parts reassociates the float sums, so outputs are not bit-identical and the exported pairwise_ranker.onnx changes. The Java side loads that artifact."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "multilingual-search/ext/evaluate.py::search", "what_it_does": "For one query group, takes the query text from the group (last row wins), posts one hybrid search request and appends one record per hit to the global responses list.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [12, 13], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "Replace the loop with query = query_group['query'].iloc[-1]; the loop leaves the last row's value, so the value and type match. Removes one pandas Series per group inside a Python loop. Groups are never empty; search has one caller, line 75, which discards its None return."}]}
{"step": 2, "unit": "multilingual-search/ext/evaluate.py::main", "what_it_does": "Parses arguments, opens a requests Session with retries, reads the query TSV, runs search over each query group in order and writes the hits to a .run file.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [75], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Parallel groupby.apply would reorder the appends to the global list unless results are re-collected in order, and requests.Session is not documented thread-safe. The gain is wall-clock only; energy benefit is not established."}]}
