{"step": 2, "unit": "examples/workshop-ecommerce/preprocess.py::process_orders", "what_it_does": "Groups order_products by order, merges the aggregated product lists onto the filtered orders for each eval set and writes orders_<eval_set>.jsonl with at most limit documents in sample mode.", "decisions": [{"label": "a", "lines": [234], "verdict": "rejected", "why": "Removing the second orders.csv read needs a frame shared through main(), a signature change beyond this unit. Moving the read into main would fail before the Processing users print on a missing file, changing printed output on that path."}, {"label": "b", "lines": [236, 237, 238, 239, 240, 241, 242, 243, 244, 245, 246, 247, 248, 249, 250, 251, 252, 253, 254, 255, 256, 257, 258, 259, 260, 261, 262, 263, 264, 265, 266, 267, 268, 269, 270, 271, 272], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "In sample mode the groupby with a Python list lambda runs over all eval-set rows (about 1.4M for train) when only limit orders are written. Repair: in sample mode keep df_order_filtered.head(limit) and filter df_order_products to those order_ids before the sort and groupby. The left merge keeps left order and the right side is unique by order_id, so the first limit rows are identical. Printed totals unchanged. Called only from main at line 412."}, {"label": "c", "lines": [290, 291, 292, 293, 294, 295, 296, 297, 298, 299, 300, 301, 302, 303, 304, 305], "verdict": "accepted", "why": "iterrows builds one pandas Series per row; itertuples with attribute access gives the same int, float and notna conversions and the same text, so the written file is identical. Gains mostly in full mode, since sample mode stops after limit rows."}]}
